## What Problem This Solves

Fixes an issue where a blank `OPENCLAW_APNS_RELAY_TIMEOUT_MS` silently overrode `gateway.push.apns.relay.timeoutMs`. A configured timeout such as `2500` was replaced with the global `10000` millisecond default, changing relay request timing without a startup error.

## Why This Change Was Made

The environment value is normalized before choosing between the existing env and config sources. Nonblank env precedence, timeout parsing and clamping, the public config shape, and the `10000` millisecond default are unchanged.

## User Impact

Operators can rely on their configured APNs relay timeout when the environment variable is blank, while valid environment overrides continue to win.

## Evidence

On Node `v24.18.0`, I invoked the real exported `resolveApnsRelayConfigFromEnv()` with a blank env override and configured timeout `2500`. Unmodified `upstream/main` (`f98bcb7fa73`) returned the wrong timeout:

```json
{
  "ok": true,
  "value": {
    "baseUrl": "https://relay.example.com",
    "timeoutMs": 10000
  }
}
```

After this change, the same production resolver preserves all three precedence states:

```json
{
  "blankEnv": {
    "ok": true,
    "value": { "baseUrl": "https://relay.example.com", "timeoutMs": 2500 }
  },
  "nonblankEnv": {
    "ok": true,
    "value": { "baseUrl": "https://relay.example.com", "timeoutMs": 1000 }
  },
  "bothAbsent": {
    "ok": true,
    "value": { "baseUrl": "https://relay.example.com", "timeoutMs": 10000 }
  }
}
```

The nonblank `999` env value is intentionally clamped by the existing minimum to `1000`.

Focused validation:

```text
node scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/infra/push-apns.relay.test.ts
Test Files  1 passed (1)
Tests       23 passed (23)
```

Replacing only `push-apns.relay.ts` with the real `upstream/main` version made the new blank-override test fail with `received 10000, expected 2500`. `CI=1 PNPM_CONFIG_VERIFY_DEPS_BEFORE_RUN=false node scripts/check-changed.mjs --staged` also passed, including core/core-test type checks, targeted lint, formatting, runtime guards, and import-cycle checks. Autoreview reported no accepted/actionable findings.

No live APNs relay request was sent; the source precedence and timeout value are fully resolved before network I/O.

AI-assisted: Codex helped implement and review the change; the production-entry proof and validation commands above were run manually.

## What Problem This Solves

Plugin authors and channel extensions currently duplicate reply-session initialization conflict classification using ad hoc regex and manual error.message checks. Without a shared, stable classifier, any change to the error message breaks hard-to-find classification logic across Discord, Signal, Slack, Telegram, and WhatsApp. The Plugin SDK should expose a single predicate that extensions import and trust.

## Summary

- expose `isReplySessionInitConflictError(error: unknown)` through
  `openclaw/plugin-sdk/error-runtime`;
- move the conflict error identity into a lightweight shared module;
- replace duplicated classification logic in Discord, Signal, Slack,
  Telegram, and WhatsApp;
- update the Plugin SDK API baseline, surface budget, and subpath docs.

## Behavior

The shared predicate recognizes:

- `ReplySessionInitConflictError` instances;
- strictly matched legacy conflict messages;
- raw legacy message strings;
- wrapped conflicts in `cause` and `error` chains;
- compatible error-like objects with an exact message.

It intentionally does not classify name-only or partial-message matches.

This normalizes wrapped-error handling and strict message matching across
channels. Retry counts, backoff, terminal behavior, and user-facing messages
are unchanged (Discord retry was already removed upstream; wrapping now
correctly routes to terminal notice).

## Evidence

### Unit tests

- classifier tests: 25/25 (typed error, legacy message, raw string,
  cause-chain wrapping, error-like objects, no-throw contract, Proxy traps);
- Plugin SDK export-contract tests: 6/6;
- Discord: 138/138 (includes new wrapped-conflict test);
- Signal: 7/7;
- Slack: 13/13;
- WhatsApp: 54/54;
- Telegram: 115/118 (3 pre-existing Windows path-separator failures).

### Static checks

- `pnpm tsgo:core` — ✅;
- `pnpm check:test-types` — ✅;
- `pnpm build:plugin-sdk:strict-smoke` — ✅ (4/4 exports);
- `pnpm plugin-sdk:api:check` — ✅;
- `pnpm plugin-sdk:surface:check` — ✅ (8150 public / 4534 callable);
- `pnpm deadcode:knip` — ✅;
- `pnpm deadcode:exports` — ✅;
- `pnpm check:import-cycles` — 0 cycles;
- `pnpm check:madge-import-cycles` — 0 cycles;
- `npx oxfmt --check` — 15 files correct;
- `git diff --check` — clean.

Four unrelated Windows-only failures were reproduced unchanged on the
upstream/main baseline: one SQLite EBUSY cleanup failure and three Telegram
path-separator assertions.

## Scope

- no retry or backoff changes;
- no terminal-notice changes;
- no new error code;
- no changes to PR #108899;
- QQBot migration is deferred until #108899 is merged.

## Summary

- add dynamic configured-model choices to the native `/model` command
- enable the existing command argument menu path for `/model` so Slack can render model picks with its existing buttons/static_select/external_select machinery
- keep bare `/model` status behavior when no configured model choices are available

## Details

`/think` already gets rich Slack command UI because its registry entry declares choices plus an argument menu. `/model` had a positional argument but no choices/menu metadata, so Slack had nothing to render before dispatching the command as plain text.

This change keeps the fix in the shared command registry: configured catalog entries become `provider/model` choices, and the existing `/model <provider/model>` setter handles the selected value. No Slack-specific command renderer changes are needed.

## Verification

- `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/auto-reply/commands-registry.test.ts`
- `git diff --check HEAD~1..HEAD`
- `node --import tsx - <<'EOF' ...` registry smoke shown below

## Real behavior proof

Behavior addressed: Slack native slash-command UX for `/model` can now surface the existing command argument menu instead of requiring users to type a model id manually.

Real environment tested: local OpenClaw source checkout in an isolated worktree from `origin/main`, running the actual command registry module after this patch.

Exact steps or command run after this patch: ran this local terminal command against the patched checkout:

```bash
node --import tsx - <<'EOF'
import { findCommandByNativeName, resolveCommandArgMenu } from './src/auto-reply/commands-registry.ts';
const command = findCommandByNativeName('model');
if (!command) throw new Error('missing /model command');
const menu = resolveCommandArgMenu({
  command,
  cfg: {
    models: {
      providers: {
        openai: { models: [{ id: 'gpt-5.5', name: 'GPT-5.5' }] },
        anthropic: { models: [{ id: 'claude-sonnet-4.6', name: 'Claude Sonnet 4.6' }] },
      },
    },
  },
});
console.log(JSON.stringify({ arg: menu?.arg.name, choices: menu?.choices }, null, 2));
EOF
```

Evidence after fix: terminal output from the patched OpenClaw registry smoke:

```json
{
  "arg": "model",
  "choices": [
    {
      "value": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-4.6",
      "label": "anthropic/Claude Sonnet 4.6"
    },
    {
      "value": "openai/gpt-5.5",
      "label": "openai/GPT-5.5"
    }
  ]
}
```

Observed result after fix: `/model` now resolves a native command argument menu for configured model choices, using `provider/model` values that the existing `/model <provider/model>` setter dispatch path already accepts. Focused registry tests also passed 31/31.

What was not tested: live Slack Block Kit interaction against a real Slack workspace. The Slack plugin path reused here is already covered by existing arg-menu render/dispatch tests; this PR adds the missing registry metadata that feeds that path.

## Notes

AI-assisted with human-requested agent workflow. The local commit helper hydrated ignored `node_modules` in the isolated worktree before committing; no tracked dependency or lockfile changes are included.

